  "query_preview": "..\nFriday through Thursday: A very active zonal jet stretching\nacross the entire Pacific Ocean will be aimed at Washington and\nsouthern British Columbia over the next seven days. The jet \nstream by the time it reaches the region will take on a \nsouthwest-northeast orientation with multiple rounds of \nprecipitation. With the jet orientation and it being aimed at \nWashington and southern BC, the heaviest precipitation will be \nfocused over the Cascades, Olympic Mountains, Vancouver Island,\nand southern BC, with occasional sloshes of precipitation \nmaking it further east into Central and Eastern Washington and\nNorth Idaho with passing weather systems embedded within the \njet. Timing of precipitation looks to pass through Friday into \nFriday Night, Saturday Night into Sunday, Monday Night through \nWednesday, with potentially another later next week. The \nmultiple rounds of precipitation in the Cascades will be \nsomething to monitor and with some uncertainty on the precise \nplacement of atmospheric rivers next week there is uncertainty \nwith rain amounts. Over the 7 day period, the GFS and ECMWF \nensembles are carrying 2-5 inches of precipitation along the \nCascade crest, around 0.15-0.50 inches in Central WA, and \n0.25-1.00 inch across NE WA into the ID Panhandle. If the higher\namounts verify for the Cascades there would be an increased \nrisk of debris flows or rockslides near burn scars in areas of \nsteep terrain. The NBM currently shows the system for Tuesday \nbeing the wettest with a 45 percent chance of rain amounts \nexceeding 1.50 inches on some burn scars near the Cascade crest.\n\nThe active southwest jet will result in above normal\ntemperatures throughout the 7 day forecast. Highs will be mainly\nin the 50s, with a few days possibly reaching the low 60s while\nin the warm sector of these passing systems (Saturday and\nWednesday). The pattern also had the potential for a few breezy\ndays, including Sunday with a weak cold front passage. This cold\nfront will briefly lower snow levels down to 4500 feet with NBM\ncarrying a 65 percent chance of 2 inches or more of snow at\nWashington Pass, and a 10 percent chance of 4 inches. JW",
